<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Santoña</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Santoña is a coastal town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabria">Cantabria</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, on the estuary of the Asón river, known for its anchovy canning and its fortifications. In August 1937, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>, Basque troops surrendered there to Italian forces fighting for Franco under the Santoña Agreement, hoping for safe passage abroad, but most were handed over to the Nationalists.</p></div>
